<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- What the window shows before the BEAM has drawn a frame.
     It was @android:color/black, which read as the app failing to start on a
     boot that takes a second or two.

     This is the SECOND thing that draws the launch logo, and until now it drew
     it at a different size from the first. Android 12+ shows its own splash for
     every app targeting 31+ (Kati is at 36) and scales the adaptive launcher
     icon into a 288dp canvas; this drawable then took over and drew the SAME
     @mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground at its natural 108dp. The mark is 0.3889 of
     that canvas either way, so it fell from 112dp to 42dp about a second in and
     stayed small for the rest of the boot — measured on a Pixel 9a as 184x294px
     then 69x110px.

     So the mark here is @drawable/kati_mark at its intrinsic 133dp, which is
     112dp of ink: the same size the system splash gives the same artwork. The
     handoff is now invisible.

     Deliberately NOT solved by deferring the first frame until the BEAM has a
     tree. That is what androidx.core:core-splashscreen does, and it would show
     one splash rather than two, but Mob's mob_start_beam polls
     Activity.hasWindowFocus() before erl_start() and hasWindowFocus() only goes
     true once the window has DRAWN (mob_beam.zig:460). Holding the frame
     starves that poll: it burned its full 3000ms timeout and then started the
     BEAM anyway, which is exactly the hwui/ERTS pthread race the wait exists to
     prevent. Matching the sizes costs nothing and keeps the BEAM's start on
     its intended signal. -->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background" />
    <item android:drawable="@drawable/kati_mark" android:gravity="center" />
</layer-list>
